{"kind":"request","text":"Operator-directed: put publishing discipline in place for the repository. Two gaps observed at main 33fa043b5d7ca696c8ab205b3d7ef8009c15016a: (1) there is no .gitignore, and the demo-built ./tailapp binary sits untracked in the repo root of every working tree (scripts/demo.sh builds it in place), inviting accidental commits of build products; (2) there is no CI — the test/race/vet/demo battery exists only as review-time convention, so nothing on the hosted repository (github.com/generalbusiness-ai/tailapp) verifies a pushed head. Requested: a .gitignore covering at minimum the built tailapp binary and other build/run droppings the demo and engine produce (e.g. temporary TAILAPP_HOME dirs if any land in-tree), and a small CI suite (GitHub Actions is the natural fit for the origin) that runs the established battery — go test ./..., go test -race ./..., go vet ./..., and scripts/demo.sh — on pushes and pull requests to main, pinned to the module Go version. Keep it small: no release automation, no lint expansion, no coverage gates; those can be later requests. Follow the usual flow: implement on a branch, publish exact-head artifacts, and request checker review.","body":{"conditions":"At an exact reviewed head: a .gitignore exists that ignores the demo-built tailapp binary (git status is clean after ./scripts/demo.sh in a fresh checkout); a CI workflow exists that runs go test ./..., go test -race ./..., go vet ./..., and ./scripts/demo.sh on push and pull_request to main with a pinned Go toolchain matching go.mod; both are documented briefly (README or docs); builder publishes exact-head artifacts for the changed paths and files a review request to checker, and a checker review verdict is filed on that head.","to":"00721374a520eabbbd4ded64dd018110f47545ff20112bee83c75f478e15f818"}}